my name is Nikki ander I'm the executive  chef of Martina pizzeria here in the  East Village where a fine casual Roman  inspired pizzeria that's open from 11:00  a.m. until late into the night when I  was a student in Rome I was studying our  history and I kept yet distracted by  trapped areas pizzerias I just loved how  cheap and easy it was to get hold of  delicious delicious pizza just at any  time of the day so the pizza Martina is  definitely inspired by this thin crust  Roman style very different in the  Neapolitan style which has a puff yard  crust I'm a big fan of crispier pizzas  things that are touched thinner you have  to be really delicate with the thin  crust pizza you can't over top it but  you don't want to under top it either I  think it's kind of like this the speed  of physics where you're getting this  dough to kind of support everything on  every single bite and then the topping  is really shine and you can actually  like crush two or three of them in a  sitting  so the way that we make our feet set  Martina is we first take the dough out  of the refrigerator we ferment at cold  temperatures that we can do a long slow  fermentation we let it come up to room  temperature and that allows the dough to  sorta come back to life a little bit  we're looking for flavor more than  anything else with our fermentation not  necessarily air pockets so we roll it  with a rolling pin until it gets very  flat about ten inches in diameter then  we take the sauce about two ounces of  sauce not too much and then just evenly  spread it all the way to the edge and  make sure the center doesn't get super  heavy with sauce once that sauce  foundation was laid down then we take  our really simple toppings if we're  doing a sausage pizza crumbled small  bits of sausage a light sprinkling of  cremini mushrooms and then two kinds of  cheese pecorino Toscano and mozzarella  or two are the ones that we mix a lot  you get a lot of that Tang from the  pecorino you get the sweetness from the  mozzarella pop it in the oven we're  using an oven that we cook at about 700  degrees for these pizzas we don't want  to cook it too too hot we like a fully  cooked dough that spends a little bit  more time on the floor instead of it  spending like 60 to 90 seconds we're  actually spending like a full two  minutes try to hit it on like a spot  that's a little bit away from the flame  cook it there for about a minute and a  half and then give it like 30 seconds of  a kiss like right by that hot fire  doesn't sound like a big difference in  time with that extra 30 seconds to a  minute actually accepts the sear both on  the top and also on the bottom then we  pull them out and this is a really  important part as we land it directly  onto a rack so that the underside of the  piece of that upskirt has a chance to  breathe a little bit and allow some that  steam to escape so you get that nice  crispy bottom we always check the  bottoms you always see me like  fanatically checking the upskirt you  lift the pizza up and look underneath to  make sure that everything is it's  looking good and evenly charred there  then you go ahead and slice it into four  pieces and give it to the lead  [Music]  so we're open till about 1:00 a.m. on  the weekends and we see a fair amount of  people both in the industry and also in  the neighborhood that are looking for a  quick bite before they go to bed so you  know between 11:00 p.m. and 1:00 a.m.  people are strolling in grabbing a pie  maybe two or three and a half bottle of  champagne I'd like the transition of  going from fine dining to this fine  casual atmosphere from the first day  that we opened I felt like we had  neighbors coming in that were already  regulars by the second week cuz when a  small space and you can see all four  corners of room you can see every face  every night you get to know people a  little quicker Martina we're calling a  fine casual pizzeria and what does that  exactly mean it means that when people  come in this door they want to have the  same feeling of hospitality that they  get when they walk into any fine-dining  restaurant
 

